findings.body:
The consolidated loop iterates `for (int j = 0; j <= keys_result->numkeys; j++)`. That's one iteration too many, and it's the root problem here.

On the last iteration `j == numkeys`, so it reads `keys_result->keys[numkeys]`, which is past the valid range. It then writes `slot_buffer[numkeys]`, past the end of the `malloc(sizeof(int) * numkeys)` allocation. That's a heap out-of-bounds write, and ASan would flag it.

The read is also a logic bug. `GETKEYS_RESULT_INIT` zero-fills `keysbuf` past the first element, so for a single-key command `keys[1].pos == 0` and the loop hashes `argv[0]`, the command name, as a second key. Its slot almost never matches the real key's slot, so a plain single-key command in cluster mode returns `CLUSTER_CROSSSLOT` and gets rejected.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1110-1123"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This affects every keyed command in cluster mode, not just edge cases. Existing cluster tests should fail immediately. Please run `tests/unit/cluster/` before any further review.

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1107-1123"}
    int first_slot = INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT;
    for (int j = 0; j < keys_result->numkeys; j++) {
        robj *this_key = argv[keys_result->keys[j].pos];
        int this_slot = (int)keyHashSlot((char*)this_key->ptr, sdslen(this_key->ptr));
        if (first_slot == INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT)
            first_slot = this_slot;
        else if (first_slot != this_slot)
            return CLUSTER_CROSSSLOT;
    }
    return first_slot;

`extractSlotFromKeysResult()` now calls `malloc()` on every keyed command in cluster mode, even though `slot_buffer` is only written and never read. That's a pointless allocation and free on the hot path, and it's the main reason the loop bug above can corrupt memory.

The `malloc` result is also unchecked. The code would write through a NULL pointer on allocation failure.

There's a related latent issue. `getSlotFromCommand()` previously handled negative `numkeys` (the result of a failed key extraction) by returning NOKEYS, with no allocation. Now the `numkeys == 0` guard doesn't catch negatives, so `malloc(sizeof(int) * numkeys)` is called with a huge size_t. It fails by accident, not by design.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1107-1123"}

::callout{type="perf"}
The buffer serves no purpose. Removing it fixes the performance cost, the unchecked NULL, and the negative-count path in one change. If the author wants a guard for negatives, use `numkeys <= 0` in the early return.

The branch was changed from `num_keys > 0` to `num_keys >= 0`. After the earlier `num_keys < 0` return, that makes the `else if` equivalent to a plain `else`. The inner cross-slot check is correct, but the condition reads as if it's doing something.

::diff{file="src/server.c" lines="4138-4148"}

Use a plain `else`, or drop the condition.
